      "content": "/**\n * The account pool: which local account should be billed, and why.\n *\n * 参考：本仓库既有的「按区域隔离的账号/凭据栈」(`src/auth.ts` 的\n *   `WorkBuddyCredentialStore`) 与「真实体积探测」(`src/probe.ts` 的\n *   `probeModel`)。两者都被直接复用，不另起一套账号或探测机制。\n * 改动：把「选哪个账号」这件事从**跟随桌面端登录**扩展为「可选的、按规则\n *   自动挑选」。这是插件原先刻意不做的事 —— `src/auth.ts` 的 `preferred()`\n *   注释写着 \"This is NOT credit-seeking\"。本次把它做成**用户显式开启**的\n *   功能，因此所有规则都在这里显式化，且永不悄悄改写用户的选择记录。\n *\n * 本模块只做**纯逻辑**（打分、排序、挑免费模型），不碰网络、不碰文件系统，\n * 以便单测直接钉住这些规则 —— 它们包含三个猜不出来、必须显式表达的结论：\n *\n *   1. **`creditMultiplier` 缺失不等于免费。** 上游的倍率来自 `credits`\n *      字符串（`x0.00`），由 `parseCreditMultiplier()` 在刷新时解析。国内版\n *      的静态 fallback 目录**整份都没有**该字段，所以「没有倍率」在国内版是\n *      常态。把 `undefined` 当作 0 会让插件在首次刷新前把收费模型当成免费\n *      模型去反复探测，直接产生用户没预期的开销。\n *   2. **没有免费模型时不回退到收费模型。** 宁可让按钮告知「本区域暂无免费\n *      模型」，也不静默换一个收费模型去跑批量测试。\n *   3. **`rate-limited` 只是暂时出池。** 它恰恰是本功能最想利用的状态\n *      （模型本身没问题），若当成永久不可用剔除，就等于把好账号扔了。\n *\n * @module dsh-connect-workbuddy/account-pool\n */\n\nimport type { WorkBuddyModelInfo } from './catalog.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyProbeOutcome } from './probe.ts'\n\n/**\n * Which of the pool's writers produced a measurement.\n *\n * `test-batch` — the user pressed \"test\" (or the check-in batch), so the\n *   outcome is a deliberate measurement of the target model.\n * `live-request` — a real chat request failed and its failure was recorded so\n *   the NEXT request starts from a measurement instead of rediscovering the\n *   same wall.\n *\n * The distinction is diagnostic, not behavioural: both feed the same routing\n * and cooling logic. It exists so a user who watched an account measure `ok`\n * and later saw it limited can tell whether that was a later live failure\n * (expected) or an older value winning (a bug).\n */\nexport type WorkBuddyPoolProbeSource = 'test-batch' | 'live-request'\n\n/** One account's pool entry: what the picker needs, plus its last measurement. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyPoolAccount {\n  /** Stable account id (the same one `WorkBuddyAccountChoice` carries). */\n  id: string\n  /** Human name, or `''` when the desktop app recorded none. */\n  accountName: string\n  /**\n   * This account belongs to an enterprise/education tenant (issue #124/#129).\n   *\n   * A projection fact, carried from the credential's `enterpriseId` rather than\n   * inferred from any benefit or check-in response: upstream can advertise\n   * campaigns and `active:false` for enterprise members while refusing every\n   * write with `10001`.\n   */\n  enterprise?: boolean\n}\n\n/**\n * What the pool knows about ONE account's last test of the target model.\n *\n * `undefined` means \"never tested\", which is deliberately distinct from every\n * measured outcome: an untested account is a candidate (the user may want to\n * use it now) whereas a measured failure is not. Collapsing the two would make\n * a fresh pool look empty until the first timer tick.\n */\nexport interface WorkBuddyPoolProbe {\n  outcome: WorkBuddyProbeOutcome\n  /** When this measurement was taken, in epoch ms. */\n  atMs: number\n  /**\n   * WHAT WROTE THIS MEASUREMENT.\n   *\n   * Two sites write probes for the same account, and before this field they\n   * were indistinguishable in the file — which made a real confusion\n   * unanswerable: the user presses \"test\", sees an account measured `ok`, and\n   * later finds it `rate-limited`. Nothing in the record said whether that was\n   * a NEWER measurement (a live request really did hit the limit) or an OLDER\n   * one somehow winning, and the file keeps no history to tell them apart.\n   *\n   * With the source recorded, that question has an answer on sight: a\n   * `live-request` entry that replaced a `test-batch` result is the pool\n   * working as designed (a live failure steers the next request away from the\n   * account); two entries of the SAME source with the OLDER one winning would\n   * be a genuine bug.\n   *\n   * ABSENT for any measurement written before this field existed, so old probe\n   * files stay readable — an absent source means \"unknown\", never a guess.\n   */\n  source?: WorkBuddyPoolProbeSource\n  /**\n   * The upstream's reset timestamp, interpreted according to the outcome.\n   *\n   * Present for a `rate-limited` outcome whose failure body carried a reset\n   * sentence, and for an `out-of-credit` outcome with a known quota refresh.\n   * For credit exhaustion this is package metadata, not a minimum wait or\n   * proof of replenishment; later positive balance evidence clears the failure.\n   * ABSENT when the upstream named no time — that absence is meaningful and\n   * must never be turned into a locally invented countdown.\n   */\n  retryAtMs?: number\n  /**\n   * The upstream's own words on the failure, already redacted.\n   *\n   * Kept so the card can say WHY an account left the pool instead of repeating\n   * one generic sentence for a DNS failure, a gateway 502 and a request the user\n   * cancelled alike. Those three used to render as the SAME line, which left the\n   * user with a message they could not act on and no way to tell a broken\n   * account from a broken network.\n   *\n   * ABSENT for a success, and for any measurement written before this field\n   * existed: an `outcome` without a `message` is a complete, valid record, so\n   * old probe files stay readable rather than being discarded.\n   */\n  message?: string\n  /**\n   * WHICH MODEL this measurement is about (issue #61).\n   *\n   * The pool's \"test all accounts\" action probes ONE model — the region's\n   * `targetModelId` — while `6004` is an ACCOUNT × MODEL condition: the reporter\n   * measured two accounts where `hy4-preview-f` answered and `step-5-preview`\n   * returned `429 / 6004` at the same moment. A row rendered as plain \"可用\"\n   * therefore claims more than the measurement says, and the pool's own routing\n   * reads that `ok` as an account-level fact.\n   *\n   * Storing the model id is what makes the claim checkable: the card can say\n   * WHICH model an `ok` came from, and a reader can see that a different model\n   * was never tested at all. `probeModel()` has always returned this field; the\n   * write simply dropped it.\n   *\n   * Deliberately NOT inherited by {@link mergePoolProbe} when an update omits it\n   * — the opposite of `accountName` and `retryAtMs`. Those two describe the\n   * ACCOUNT (a name that a later writer could not see is still true, and a\n   * future refresh time is still in the future), whereas the model describes\n   * THIS measurement. Carrying an older model id forward would relabel a fresh\n   * measurement with a model it was not taken on, which is exactly the confusion\n   * this field exists to end. ABSENT therefore means \"unknown scope\", never a\n   * guess.\n   */\n  modelId?: string\n  /**\n   * The account's display name AT THE MOMENT IT WAS MEASURED (issue #60).\n   *\n   * Stored because a saved pool member can outlive its sign-in. Once the\n   * desktop app drops a login, that id resolves to no listed account, so the\n   * card has nothing left to render for it — and the old warning (\"1 saved\n   * account is no longer signed in\") was a count with no subject, leaving the\n   * user unable to tell WHICH account had gone. The name was in hand at write\n   * time (both writers already carry it) and nowhere afterwards, so the record\n   * is the only place it can survive.\n   *\n   * ABSENT on records written before this field existed, and when the desktop\n   * app recorded no name: the card then falls back to a short id prefix rather\n   * than inventing a label. Snapshots are never refreshed from a LISTING — only\n   * a real measurement writes this, so a stale name cannot be \"confirmed\" by a\n   * routine scan that never saw the account.\n   */\n  accountName?: string\n}\n\n/** One account's live credit situation, as the pool ranks it. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyPoolCredits {\n  total: number\n  /** Credits expiring within the card's \"soon\" window (3 days). */\n  expiringSoon: number\n  /** When the nearest package expires, if any does. */\n  nearestExpiryMs?: number\n}\n\n/**\n * Everything the ranking rule reads about one account. Assembled by the Host\n * from the credential store, the credits route, and the runtime probe store —\n * this module only consumes it.\n */\nexport interface WorkBuddyPoolMember {\n  account: WorkBuddyPoolAccount\n  credits?: WorkBuddyPoolCredits\n  probe?: WorkBuddyPoolProbe\n  /** When the stored credential expires, used as the final tie-break. */\n  tokenExpiresAtMs?: number\n}\n\n/**\n * Why one account was excluded from being billed.\n *\n * Reported per account rather than collapsed into a single \"no candidate\"\n * answer, because the three reasons call for completely different actions\n * (wait / re-auth / top up), and the card has to say which one applies.\n */\nexport type WorkBuddyPoolExclusion =\n  | 'unusable'\n  | 'rate-limited'\n  | 'model-busy'\n  | 'out-of-credit'\n  | 'credential-rejected'\n  /**\n   * The upstream restricted this credential for unusual activity (issue #127).\n   *\n   * Unlike `policy-rejected`, this is an account-level decision. Another\n   * account may serve and this one should not be billed again until the user\n   * re-authenticates or appeals successfully.\n   */\n  | 'account-restricted'\n  | 'not-found'\n  | 'unavailable'\n  | 'failed'\n\n/** One ranked account: its score, and whether it can serve right now. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyPoolRanked {\n  account: WorkBuddyPoolAccount\n  /** Higher wins. Only comparable between rows of the same ranking call. */\n  score: number\n  /** Absent when the account is a candidate; set when it is excluded. */\n  excludedBy?: WorkBuddyPoolExclusion\n}\n\n/**\n * How long an account stays out of the pool when the upstream stated no time.\n *\n * A conservative FALLBACK, not a measurement: it applies whenever a failed\n * outcome carries no `retryAtMs`, which is every rate limit that came without a\n * reset sentence AND every transient failure (a transport error, a 5xx). In both\n * cases \"when can I use this again\" has no honest answer.\n *\n * The two failure directions it avoids: keeping the account in the pool would\n * re-bill it immediately and re-trip the same limit; dropping it FOREVER would\n * discard a working account over an event that said nothing about it. A short,\n * stated cooldown is the least-wrong of the two — and it is what lets the pool\n * heal itself, since nothing else re-probes an excluded member.\n */\nexport const POOL_UNKNOWN_COOLDOWN_MS = 30 * 60_000\n\n/**\n * How long a MODEL-scoped throttle benches its account.\n *\n * Short on purpose, and THIS is the fix for issue #56.\n *\n * `model_busy` (upstream 14003) is not a statement about the account at all: it\n * is the model's own load, and the reporter's measurement shows the same\n * credential answering other models at the same second. Benching the account for\n * the generic half-hour fallback therefore did three wrong things at once:\n *\n * 1. It waited 30x longer than the upstream's own advice (\"wait about a\n *    minute\").\n * 2. It benched EVERY account, because failover walked the pool and each member\n *    hit the same model-level wall — so the user could not talk to the model at\n *    all for half an hour.\n * 3. It taught the pool a false fact about each account, overwriting a good\n *    `test-batch` measurement with a `live-request` failure that said nothing\n *    about the account.\n *\n * The value is deliberately the same order as {@link MODEL_BUSY_COOLDOWN_MS} in\n * `src/probe.ts`, but it is NOT imported from there: this module is deliberately\n * dependency-light (it is the pure ranking rule), and a cooldown that a probe\n * writes and a ranking reads must not be able to drift by a refactor of either\n * file. A unit test pins the two together instead.\n */\nexport const MODEL_BUSY_COOLDOWN_MS = 2 * 60_000\n\n/**\n * Whether one account can be billed at `nowMs`.\n *\n * Returns the exclusion reason, or undefined when the account is a candidate.\n * The order matters and is NOT arbitrary:\n *\n * - A `credential-rejected` account is excluded regardless of any cooldown: its\n *   token is not accepted, so waiting cannot help and the user must re-auth.\n * - A limited, unreachable or failed outcome is checked against its stated (or\n *   fallback) cooldown, so an account whose limit has already reset — or whose\n *   gateway blipped once — is back in the pool without needing another test\n *   first.\n * - An `ok` measurement, and no measurement at all, are both candidates. The\n *   latter matters: a freshly discovered account has never been tested and must\n *   not be invisible.\n *\n * WHY TRANSIENT OUTCOMES GET A COOLDOWN. They are not statements about the\n * ACCOUNT. A transport failure can be a DNS blip, and — before the shim learned\n * to skip reporting an aborted request — it was routinely the user closing the\n * panel mid-flight. A 5xx is the gateway's afternoon, not this account's health.\n * Excluding them permanently meant one transient event idled a perfectly good\n * account until the user happened to press \"test\" again, and with every member\n * so idled the pool had no candidate at all: failover then had nowhere to go and\n * the raw upstream error was reported as-is. The cooldown is what makes the pool\n * RECOVER instead of staying broken.\n *\n * `not-found` and `credential-rejected` deliberately get none: one describes a\n * model the region no longer offers (a catalog fact, not a transient one) and\n * the other needs the user to sign in again. Neither is fixed by waiting, and\n * pretending otherwise would re-bill a token the upstream has already refused.\n *\n * Each unusable outcome keeps its OWN value rather than being folded into\n * `credential-rejected`. The fold made one row state two contradictory things —\n * the name column said \"被拒绝\" (sign in again) while the probe column, reading\n * the same measurement, said \"连不上上游——这是网络问题，不是模型问题\" — and it\n * told a user whose upstream had merely blipped that their sign-in was bad.\n * (That label has since been made cause-NEUTRAL: it used to assert \"not the\n * model\" for a bucket that also holds gateway 5xx and request timeouts, where\n * the claim is simply unfounded. The recorded `message` now carries the cause.)\n * The distinctions are already computed upstream in `outcomeOfFailure`\n * (`src/probe.ts:290-299`); this only stops discarding them.\n */\nexport function exclusionOf(\n  probe: WorkBuddyPoolProbe | undefined,\n  nowMs: number,\n): WorkBuddyPoolExclusion | undefined {\n  if (probe === undefined) return undefined\n  switch (probe.outcome) {\n    case 'ok':\n      return undefined\n    case 'credential-rejected':\n      return 'credential-rejected'\n    case 'account-restricted':\n      // The upstream explicitly identifies this credential as restricted, so a\n      // cooldown cannot repair it: the user must re-auth/appeal. Keep it out of\n      // failover rather than rotating through every known dead account (issue\n      // #127).\n      return 'account-restricted'\n    case 'not-found':\n      return 'not-found'\n    case 'unavailable':\n      return retryDue(probe, nowMs) ? undefined : 'unavailable'\n    case 'failed':\n      return retryDue(probe, nowMs) ? undefined : 'failed'\n    case 'rate-limited':\n      return retryDue(probe, nowMs) ? undefined : 'rate-limited'\n    case 'model-busy':\n      // The model is loaded, not the account. It STILL leaves the pool — a\n      // request billed to it right now would hit the same wall — but on its own\n      // short clock ({@link MODEL_BUSY_COOLDOWN_MS}), never the half-hour\n      // fallback that turned one busy minute into a pool-wide outage.\n      //\n      // NOT a permanent candidacy either: returning `undefined` here (the\n      // treatment `policy-rejected` gets) would make failover re-try the same\n      // account against a model known to be refusing it, which is exactly the\n      // \"walk every member into the same wall\" behaviour issue #56 reports.\n      return retryDue(probe, nowMs, MODEL_BUSY_COOLDOWN_MS) ? undefined : 'model-busy'\n    case 'out-of-credit':\n      // Time alone cannot establish that check-in or purchased credits arrived.\n      // A later positive balance reading clears the recorded failure.\n      return 'out-of-credit'\n    case 'policy-rejected':\n      // NOT the `default` branch. A content-policy refusal is a fact about ONE\n      // REQUEST, not about the account: the server refused that message's\n      // content, and the same account will happily serve a different one. It is\n      // therefore a candidate, exactly like `ok` — benching it would idle a\n      // healthy account over a single tripped filter, which is the failure the\n      // transient-outcome note above exists to prevent (and worse here, since\n      // the user's next request may well be perfectly acceptable).\n      //\n      // The measurement is still recorded, so the card can explain what\n      // happened; it just must not remove the account from the pool.\n      return undefined\n    case 'channel-rejected':\n      // Same treatment as `policy-rejected`, for the same structure of fact: the\n      // gateway refused the PROMPT, and every account sends that same prompt, so\n      // none of them is a better candidate. Benching this one would remove an\n      // account that has nothing wrong with it — and hide the real remedy\n      // (change the prompt, not the account).\n      return undefined\n    default:\n      // An outcome this build does not know is treated as unusable rather than\n      // as a candidate: billing through a state we cannot interpret is worse\n      // than leaving the account out until a later version explains it. It keeps\n      // the no-cooldown treatment too — a state we cannot read is not one we\n      // should re-enter on a timer.\n      return 'unusable'\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Whether an account's cooldown has elapsed (or was never stated).\n *\n * Transient failures without a stated retry time use the local short window.\n * Credit exhaustion is cleared by later positive balance evidence or an explicit\n * successful pool test; ordinary chat success does not update the probe store.\n * It does not call this helper.\n *\n * `fallbackMs` lets one outcome carry a SHORTER clock than the generic one: the\n * model-scoped throttle is upstream's own \"about a minute\", and applying the\n * half-hour account fallback to it is issue #56. It is a parameter rather than a\n * second function so every outcome keeps sharing one definition of \"due\".\n */\nfunction retryDue(\n  probe: WorkBuddyPoolProbe,\n  nowMs: number,\n  fallbackMs: number = POOL_UNKNOWN_COOLDOWN_MS,\n): boolean {\n  const until = probe.retryAtMs ?? probe.atMs + fallbackMs\n  return nowMs >= until\n}\n\n/**\n * When an EXCLUDED account becomes a candidate again, in epoch ms.\n *\n * Same arithmetic as {@link retryDue} — deliberately, so the two can never\n * disagree about when an account is back: this answers \"at what instant\", that\n * one answers \"is that instant past\". Two separate clocks would let the card\n * say \"recovers at 14:05\" while the ranking still refuses it at 14:06.\n *\n * `Infinity` when time cannot fix the account: a rejected token and a\n * restricted or banned account are states a cooldown cannot repair, so they\n * sort after everything that merely has to wait (`exclusionOf` gives them no\n * cooldown at all — this mirrors that, rather than inventing one).\n */\nfunction recoversAtOf(\n  probe: WorkBuddyPoolProbe | undefined,\n  excludedBy: WorkBuddyPoolExclusion,\n  fallbackMs: number = POOL_UNKNOWN_COOLDOWN_MS,\n): number {\n  if (probe === undefined) return Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY\n  switch (excludedBy) {\n    case 'rate-limited':\n    case 'unavailable':\n    case 'failed':\n      return probe.retryAtMs ?? probe.atMs + fallbackMs\n    case 'model-busy':\n      return probe.retryAtMs ?? probe.atMs + MODEL_BUSY_COOLDOWN_MS\n    // `out-of-credit` gets a date here even though the exclusion does not clear\n    // on the clock: `retryAtMs` for it is package metadata (when the allowance\n    // resets), which is exactly the \"when might this be usable\" fact the card\n    // wants to show. It does NOT become a candidate at that instant — a later\n    // positive balance read does that (`exclusionOf` says so).\n    case 'out-of-credit':\n      return probe.retryAtMs ?? Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY\n    default:\n      return Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Rank a pool's members best-first.\n *\n * The rule, in priority order — this is the whole feature:\n *\n * 1. **Usability.** Only accounts that can be billed right now are candidates;\n *    they always outrank every excluded one.\n * 2. **Expiring credits first, soonest first.** Points about to expire are\n *    worth exactly zero after they do, so spending them before they lapse is\n *    strictly better than saving them.\n * 3. **User's manual order.** When the user has hand-ordered the pool (see\n *    `orderAccountIds`), that order breaks ties among accounts that are equal\n *    on the keys above AND below. It is the one place the user's own preference\n *    influences \"who serves next\": the manual picker on the card drags these\n *    ids into place, and the ranking honors the result.\n *    WHY EXPIRY STILL OUTRANKS THE MANUAL ORDER: this rule sits strictly BELOW\n *    the at-risk flag (rule #2), so an account with ANY expiring credits always\n *    beats the manual order even when the user dragged it to the bottom. That\n *    is deliberate (issue #70): a manual order that is unsaved, stale, or\n *    sloppily edited must never cause credits to lapse unspent — expiring\n *    credits are real money about to vanish, while the manual order is just a\n *    preference that might be wrong. This is not a bug, and a test pins it.\n * 4. **Largest expiring amount.** Between two accounts that both have expiring\n *    points, the one with more at risk goes first.\n * 5. **Credits, highest first.** Among accounts with nothing expiring, spend the\n *    one with the most, so a nearly-drained account is conserved.\n * 6. **Freshest credential.** Tie-break so a stale token never wins a draw.\n * 7. **Account id.** Final tie-break, making the order TOTAL: two accounts\n *    equal on every ranking key would otherwise keep the caller's array order,\n *    so the same pool could rotate differently between two runs.\n *\n * WHY EXPIRY OUTRANKS SIZE (issue #70). These used to be strict lexicographic\n * keys: total, THEN expiry. Since totals are almost never exactly equal, the\n * expiry rule was unreachable — a live report had an account with 196 points\n * lapsing that day ranked LAST, behind two accounts that expire weeks later,\n * simply because it held ~1,200 fewer points overall. It was never selected, so\n * the 196 points always lapsed. A \"use it or lose it\" rule that another rule\n * completely shadows is not a tie-break, it is dead code.\n *\n * WHY SPENDING THE SMALL EXPIRING ACCOUNT IS STILL RIGHT. Ordering by expiry\n * does mean an account with 1 point lapsing outranks one holding 50,000. That\n * looks wrong and is not: credits are fungible, so using the rich account\n * instead does not preserve the 1 point — it spends the 1 point's chance and\n * loses it. Using the expiring account spends a point that was going to be lost\n * anyway, and the rich account is still there for the next request. The only\n * real cost is a failover round-trip if the expiring account cannot cover a\n * whole request, which the exclusion/failover path already absorbs. Ordering by\n * total instead loses the expiring points with certainty.\n *\n * Excluded accounts are still RETURNED (sorted after the candidates) so the\n * card can explain why each is out; they differ from candidates by\n * `excludedBy` being set on them, never by being silently dropped.\n *\n * Pure and total: the same input always yields the same order, and it never\n * throws on missing data (an account with no credits is ranked as 0, not\n * crashed on).\n */\nexport function rankPool(\n  members: readonly WorkBuddyPoolMember[],\n  nowMs: number,\n  orderAccountIds?: readonly string[],\n): WorkBuddyPoolRanked[] {\n  // The user's manual order, as a position lookup so missing ids (and accounts\n  // never listed) map consistently to \"sorted last\". Position 0 is \"first\".\n  // Duplicate ids keep the FIRST occurrence's position — hand lists often have\n  // a stray duplicate, and whichever the user intended first must win.\n  const order = new Map<string, number>()\n  for (const [index, id] of (orderAccountIds ?? []).entries()) {\n    if (!order.has(id)) order.set(id, index)\n  }\n  const rows = members.map(member => {\n    // A zero balance does not rule out free models. Keep this verdict identical\n    // to the panel and shortfall explanation: only a recorded failure excludes.\n    const excludedBy = exclusionOf(member.probe, nowMs)\n    return {\n      account: member.account,\n      score: scoreOf(member),\n      ...excludedBy === undefined ? {} : { excludedBy },\n      // Ordering keys below the primary score; not part of the public row.\n      _usable: excludedBy === undefined,\n      // The user's manual order position; Infinity = not listed = sorted last.\n      _order: order.get(member.account.id) ?? Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n      // Whether ANYTHING lapses within the window, and how much.\n      //\n      // `expiringSoon` is the amount (credits); `nearestExpiryMs` is a\n      // timestamp. Comparing them directly would be a units error, so they\n      // stay two separate keys: `_expiring` now means \"has something at risk\"\n      // (the old key reused the name for the raw timestamp, which is why the\n      // amount was never consultable at all).\n      _atRisk: (member.credits?.expiringSoon ?? 0) > 0,\n      _expiringAmount: member.credits?.expiringSoon ?? 0,\n      _expiring: member.credits?.nearestExpiryMs ?? Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n      _tokenExpiresAtMs: member.tokenExpiresAtMs ?? 0,\n      // When this account becomes a candidate again (issue #240).\n      //\n      // Only meaningful for EXCLUDED rows, and only as a DISPLAY order among\n      // them: it is computed here so the card can list \"the one that comes back\n      // soonest\" first, which is the half of the reporter's rule 2 that the\n      // ranking can honestly answer. It deliberately does not change who\n      // SERVES: an excluded row is not a candidate at any position, and when\n      // its cooldown elapses `exclusionOf` drops the exclusion and the ordinary\n      // credit keys decide — so \"prefer the account that recovers first\" is\n      // expressed as \"it is at the top of the table and explains why\", not as a\n      // sticky preference.\n      //\n      // Infinity for a candidate and for an account with no stated recovery\n      // time: `undefined` here means \"nobody can say\", and inventing a number\n      // would put an account in a position the plugin cannot justify.\n      _recoversAt: excludedBy === undefined ? Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY : recoversAtOf(member.probe, excludedBy),\n    }\n  })\n  rows.sort((left, right) => {\n    if (left._usable !== right._usable) return left._usable ? -1 : 1\n    // Among two EXCLUDED accounts: whichever recovers soonest goes first, then\n    // the richer one (issue #240).\n    //\n    // This only ever orders rows that cannot serve — `pickAccount` and the\n    // failover loop skip every `excludedBy !== undefined` row before reading\n    // its position — so it changes the TABLE, not who is billed. That is the\n    // honest scope: the reporter's \"prefer the account with the shortest\n    // remaining cooldown\" cannot mean \"bill it\", because a cooling account\n    // refuses by definition. What it can mean is that the account about to come\n    // back is the one at the top of the list, saying so, instead of being\n    // buried wherever the credit keys left it.\n    //\n    // Above `_order` deliberately: a manual rank on a row that cannot serve\n    // conveys nothing, and the user's complaint is precisely that they had to\n    // hand-sort to get a sensible order. Usable rows are unaffected — both\n    // sides carry `Infinity` there and the branch never runs.\n    if (!left._usable && !right._usable && left._recoversAt !== right._recoversAt) {\n      return left._recoversAt - right._recoversAt\n    }\n    // Expiring credits before anything else among usable accounts (issue #70).\n    if (left._atRisk !== right._atRisk) return left._atRisk ? -1 : 1\n    // The user's manual order, only among accounts equal on usability & the\n    // at-risk flag — see the doc note about why expiry still outranks it.\n    if (left._order !== right._order) return left._order - right._order\n    // Both at risk: whichever lapses FIRST goes first.\n    if (left._atRisk && right._atRisk && left._expiring !== right._expiring) {\n      return left._expiring - right._expiring\n    }\n    // Same expiry (or neither at risk): the larger amount at stake wins, which\n    // for accounts with nothing expiring falls through to total credits.\n    if (left._expiringAmount !== right._expiringAmount) {\n      return right._expiringAmount - left._expiringAmount\n    }\n    if (left.score !== right.score) return right.score - left.score\n    if (left._tokenExpiresAtMs !== right._tokenExpiresAtMs) {\n      return right._tokenExpiresAtMs - left._tokenExpiresAtMs\n    }\n    // Final key: the account id. Without it, two accounts identical on every\n    // ranking key would keep whatever order the caller happened to pass, so\n    // the same pool could rotate differently between runs — an instability\n    // that makes the feature hard to reason about and impossible to test.\n    // Ids are unique, so this makes the order TOTAL.\n    return left.account.id < right.account.id ? -1 : left.account.id > right.account.id ? 1 : 0\n  })\n  return rows.map(({ account, score, excludedBy }) => ({\n    account,\n    score,\n    ...excludedBy === undefined ? {} : { excludedBy },\n  }))\n}\n\n/**\n * The checked ids that still resolve to a local sign-in, in the caller's order.\n *\n * This is THE definition of \"effective members\", and it exists because the same\n * predicate was written five separate times: the status document\n * (`web-status.ts`), the batch route's empty-pool guard, the Host dependency the\n * card reads, `poolMemberAccounts` and the browser helper. Five copies of one\n * rule is this project's single most productive bug shape (\"fixed one end,\n * missed the other\"), so the rule now has one home and every site delegates to\n * it.\n *\n * ORDER IS THE CALLER'S, and comes from `orderedIds`: the answer preserves the\n * order of THAT argument. Callers pass whichever list's order they want to show\n * — the saved list for the user's own order, the store's roster for the account\n * table's. Neither is silently reshuffled by the filter. (The first parameter was\n * once named `saved`, which read as \"this must be the saved list\" even though\n * `web-status.ts` legitimately passes the roster for store order; the name was\n * the trap, so it now describes the ROLE instead of the source.)\n *\n * Pure, so it is unit-testable without a store, and it never mutates its input.\n */\nexport function effectiveMembersOf(\n  orderedIds: readonly string[],\n  listedIds: ReadonlySet<string>,\n): string[] {\n  return orderedIds.filter(id => listedIds.has(id))\n}\n\n/**\n * The account to bill, or undefined when the pool has no usable member.\n *\n * A thin selector over {@link rankPool} so the winner is decided in exactly one\n * place: every caller that needs \"who serves now\" gets the same answer.\n */\nexport function pickAccount(\n  members: readonly WorkBuddyPoolMember[],\n  nowMs: number,\n  orderAccountIds?: readonly string[],\n): WorkBuddyPoolAccount | undefined {\n  return rankPool(members, nowMs, orderAccountIds).find(row => row.excludedBy === undefined)?.account\n}\n\n/**\n * The credit figure used for ordering.\n *\n * Absent credits rank as zero. Deliberately NOT \"unknown ranks first\": an\n * account whose balance could not be read must never outrank one we positively\n * know is full, or a failing credits route would capture all the traffic.\n */\nfunction scoreOf(member: WorkBuddyPoolMember): number {\n  return member.credits?.total ?? 0\n}\n\n/**\n * Pick the free model to test against, from one region's catalog.\n *\n * \"Free\" means `creditMultiplier === 0` EXACTLY. An absent multiplier is NOT\n * free — see the module note: the CN static fallback carries no multiplier at\n * all, so treating absence as zero would silently bill real credits.\n *\n * Among the free candidates the LARGEST context window wins, because the probe\n * sends a real-sized payload (25k input) and a long-conversation probe is the\n * point: a small-window model would either reject it or answer a question the\n * user never asked.\n *\n * Returns undefined when the region has no free model, which the caller must\n * render as \"none available\" — never as a fallback to a paid model.\n */\nexport function pickFreeModel(\n  catalog: readonly WorkBuddyModelInfo[],\n): WorkBuddyModelInfo | undefined {\n  let best: WorkBuddyModelInfo | undefined\n  for (const model of catalog) {\n    if (model.creditMultiplier !== 0) continue\n    if (best === undefined || model.contextWindow > best.contextWindow) best = model\n  }\n  return best\n}\n\n/**\n * A latest-wins guard for one region's asynchronous work.\n *\n * Exists because `applyRotation` awaits (it fetches credits per member), so two\n * overlapping calls can interleave: a slow call that read \"rotation is ON\" can\n * resume AFTER a newer call cleared the override for \"rotation is OFF\" and write\n * the stale account back — leaving the card showing the pool switched off while\n * a rotated account is still billed.\n *\n * `begin()` claims a new generation and returns a probe; the caller must check\n * it after EVERY await and bail when it reports stale. Extracted as a pure\n * factory rather than inlined so the rule is unit-testable: an inlined token is\n * only observable by reproducing a real interleaving, which is slow and easy to\n * get subtly wrong (a harness that never actually overlaps passes either way).\n */\nexport function createLatestWins<Key extends string>(): {\n  begin(key: Key): () => boolean\n} {\n  const generations: Partial<Record<Key, number>> = {}\n  return {\n    begin(key: Key): () => boolean {\n      const generation = (generations[key] ?? 0) + 1\n      generations[key] = generation\n      return () => generations[key] !== generation\n    },\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * WHY THERE IS NO SCHEDULER HERE.\n *\n * This module used to carry `POOL_TICK_MS`, `poolDueAt` and `duePoolRegions` for\n * an automatic re-test timer. That timer was removed as a product decision\n * (`8c17fef`): rotation is a PERSISTENT change of who pays while failover is a\n * PER-REQUEST borrow of another account, and having both made \"who is being\n * billed right now\" unanswerable on one card.\n *\n * The pure helpers were left behind and kept green by their own unit tests —\n * this project's most misleading kind of dead weight, because the suite asserted\n * a capability the shipped plugin no longer had. \"All tests pass\" then said\n * nothing about whether an account ever came back.\n *\n * Recovery is expressed as the cooldown in {@link exclusionOf} instead, which\n * needs no timer: it is evaluated at ranking time, so a cooled-down member simply\n * becomes a candidate again on the next request.\n */\n\n/**\n * Where the pool's target model came from.\n *\n * `stale` is its own state rather than being folded into `none`: \"this region\n * has no free model\" and \"the model you saved is no longer offered\" need\n * opposite advice (refresh the catalog / pick another model), and collapsing\n * them would make the card say the wrong one.\n */\nexport type WorkBuddyPoolTargetSource = 'preferred' | 'free' | 'none' | 'stale'\n\n/** The resolved target model, and where the choice came from. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyPoolTarget {\n  /**\n   * The model a batch may test, present ONLY when it is safe to run.\n   *\n   * Deliberately ABSENT for `stale`: every existing guard refuses on a missing\n   * id, so omitting it means a caller that forgets to check `source` still\n   * fails safe instead of spending credits on an unverifiable model.\n   */\n  modelId?: string\n  /** The saved id that could not be resolved; present only for `stale`. */\n  staleModelId?: string\n  source: WorkBuddyPoolTargetSource\n}\n\n/**\n * Resolve the model the pool should test against.\n *\n * A user-specified id always wins (it is a preference, not a computed default),\n * and is returned even when it is a PAID model: the card says so explicitly\n * rather than silently overriding the user's choice.\n *\n * But the choice is now VALIDATED against the region's catalog, because the\n * catalog changes: the CN roster is empty until its first refresh, and models\n * come and go. A saved id that is no longer offered used to be returned as\n * `preferred`, which let the card enable its test button and the Host run a\n * batch against a model nobody offers. It is reported as `stale` instead —\n * never silently replaced by the free model (that would test something the\n * user did not choose) and never run.\n *\n * A catalog that has not loaded yet therefore reads as `stale` for a saved id.\n * That is the safe direction: no credits are spent while the roster is unknown,\n * and the card offers to switch back to automatic.\n */\nexport function resolveTargetModel(\n  catalog: readonly WorkBuddyModelInfo[],\n  preferredId: string | undefined,\n): WorkBuddyPoolTarget {\n  if (preferredId !== undefined && preferredId !== '') {\n    return catalog.some(model => model.id === preferredId)\n      ? { modelId: preferredId, source: 'preferred' }\n      : { staleModelId: preferredId, source: 'stale' }\n  }\n  const free = pickFreeModel(catalog)\n  return free === undefined ? { source: 'none' } : { modelId: free.id, source: 'free' }\n}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "src/adapter.ts",
      "content": "/**\n * The WorkBuddy pi-ai providers: loopback-backed adapters registered\n * into the Harness LLM seam, assembled from public `dsh-llm-pi-ai`\n * extension points. One instance per region — `workbuddy` for the domestic\n * gateway, `workbuddy-global` for the international one — each pointing at\n * its own shim and catalog so the two regions serve simultaneously.\n *\n * 参考：corrinehu/dsh-workbuddy-connect（MIT，Copyright (c) 2026 Corrine Hu）\n *   — pi-ai provider 的装配方式（createProvider + openAICompletionsApi +\n *     inert auth plane + 用 shim 的进程内 secret 作为 apiKey）由该项目实现；\n *   DSH 插件结构与 provider 注册的思路参照\n *     franksong2702/dsh-codex-connect（Apache-2.0），经其转引。\n * 改动：模型描述符补上 upstream 给出的多模态与推理档位信息（若有），\n *   供 DSH 的能力判断使用；工厂参数化 provider id，支持双区域实例。\n *\n * @module dsh-connect-workbuddy/adapter\n */\n\nimport { createAssistantMessageEventStream, createProvider, isContextOverflow } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai'\nimport type { Api, AssistantMessage, AssistantMessageEvent, AssistantMessageEventStream, AuthContext, Context, CredentialStore, Message, Model, Provider, ProviderStreams } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai'\nimport { openAICompletionsApi } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai/api/openai-completions.lazy'\nimport { ReasoningEffortId, resolveRetryPolicy } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm'\nimport type { GenerateOptions, LlmModelInfo, LlmModelReasoningInfo, LlmResolvedModelInfo, PreparedAdapterCall, StreamChunk } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm'\nimport { PiAiAdapter } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm-pi-ai'\nimport type { PiAiAdapterOptions, ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm-pi-ai'\nimport type { AttachmentStore } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-attachment'\nimport type { WorkBuddyCredentialStore } from './auth.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyCatalog, WorkBuddyModelInfo } from './catalog.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyShim } from './shim.ts'\nimport { offRefused } from './off-thinking.ts'\nimport { defaultEffortFor } from './default-effort.ts'\nimport { promoBadgeTone, selectPromoBadge } from './promo.ts'\nimport { WORKBUDDY_DEFAULT_EFFORT } from './default-effort.ts'\nimport { isBodyOversizeRefusal, WORKBUDDY_FALLBACK_SYSTEM_PROMPT } from './upstream.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyRegion } from './upstream.ts'\nimport { piAiRuntimeInfo } from './pi-ai-runtime.ts'\nimport type { PiAiGeneration } from './pi-ai-runtime.ts'\nimport type { ContextRescue, RescueCause } from './context-rescue.ts'\n\n/**\n * The tier a request gets when it names none — compaction summaries, above all\n * (issue #83). See the `reasoning` field in `profileFor` for why an absent\n * default is not \"no opinion\" but \"whatever pi-ai's level map says\", which is\n * how `off` became the silent default for `/compact`.\n *\n * Re-exported from `./default-effort.ts`, which is where issue #179 put the\n * per-model override: this constant is now the route-wide FALLBACK for a model\n * the user has not picked a tier for, not the final answer for the roster. The\n * name and value are unchanged so every existing caller and test still reads.\n */\nexport { WORKBUDDY_DEFAULT_EFFORT } from './default-effort.ts'\n\n/** Provider route this bundle owns for the domestic (CN) gateway. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER = 'workbuddy'\n\n/** Provider route this bundle owns for the international gateway. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER = 'workbuddy-global'\n\n/** The provider id each region registers as. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_PROVIDERS: Readonly<Record<WorkBuddyRegion, string>> = {\n  cn: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER,\n  global: WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER,\n}\n\n/**\n * Model identity BOTH regions stamp on their models — the fix for issue #59.\n *\n * pi-ai keeps a historical thinking block as `thinking` only when the message\n * that carried it names the SAME provider, api and model id as the request in\n * flight (`transform-messages.js`, `isSameModel`); every other block is\n * downgraded to plain `text` — downgraded, not dropped, which is why no byte\n * count ever explained the 1.94× regional jump. The two gateways are two\n * provider STRINGS for ONE model, so a transcript recorded in one region was\n * replayed as text in the other, and issue #59's reporter measured exactly\n * that: 708K → 1.374M on byte-identical history, and a compression request\n * that could then never fit the window it was computed for.\n *\n * (pi-ai 1.3 changed the second half: a foreign block is now DISCARDED rather\n * than rewritten as text. The identity stamp is unaffected — it is what makes\n * the block count as same-model in the first place, and that comparison is\n * unchanged in every generation this plugin declares.)\n *\n * The stamp is NOT inert, and reading it as inert is what broke issue #75.\n * `provider` is the KEY pi-ai dispatches on: `requireProvider` resolves\n * `this.providers.get(model.provider)` and throws\n * `Unknown provider: ${model.provider}` when it is absent (`models.js:410–416`\n * on the host's 1.1.0, `:351–357` on the 0.85.1 this repo resolves), and\n * `getAuth` resolves the same key, answering `Provider is not configured`\n * instead (`:334–341` and `:429`; `:275–280` and `:366` on 0.85.1). Both sit on\n * the LIVE chat path: `dsh-llm-pi-ai` streams through\n * `snapshot.models.streamSimple(model, …)` (`lib/index.js:1983`), which calls\n * `requireProvider` before a byte is sent (`models.js:454–459`). Each region\n * builds its OWN registry from its own adapter — `current()` registers\n * `profile.piProvider` per profile (`lib/index.js:1852–1862`) — so the\n * `workbuddy-global` region registered only `workbuddy-global` and could not\n * dispatch a model stamped `workbuddy`: every request failed with\n * `Unknown provider: workbuddy` before reaching the gateway.\n * {@link modelIdentityKeysOf} is the correction: the identity has to be a\n * registry key of its own in every region, or the stamp cannot be shared.\n *\n * Lookup by ROUTE is a separate question and stays intact: `getModel(provider,\n * id)` is `getModels(provider).find(...)` (`models.js:116–118`), i.e. it goes\n * through the map key, while the STAMPED field is whatever the provider put\n * there. So the route stays `workbuddy`/`workbuddy-global` (each with its own\n * shim, catalog and credential store) while every model it serves advertises\n * THIS id. The openai-completions api reads `model.provider` only for\n * provider-specific quirks (none of which match these ids) and as an\n * error-message label.\n *\n * Deliberately NOT the same string as the global route: a message is only\n * same-model when provider, api AND model id all match, so the shared id must\n * not be reachable as a route a user could pick — it names one model, not one\n * gateway. It IS reachable as a pi-ai registry key, which is the point: those\n * are internal and never surface as a route, because `registerAdapter` takes\n * the route list explicitly (`dsh-llm/lib/index.js:1842–1861`,\n * `prepareRoutes` `:1867–1885`) and this plugin passes exactly the two region\n * ids (`src/index.ts:2069–2086`).\n */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY = 'workbuddy'\n\n/**\n * The api string `dsh-llm-pi-ai` stamps on a replayed message it could not\n * rebuild natively (`foreignAssistant()`), i.e. a message that went through the\n * host's fallback path rather than this plugin's own request builder.\n *\n * It is the ONLY api value {@link alignAssistantIdentities} repairs, because it\n * is the only one that proves the field is a host marker instead of a shape this\n * plugin really produced.\n */\nexport const HOST_FOREIGN_API = 'dsh-foreign'\n\n/**\n * Registry keys an adapter serving `providerId` must publish so that every\n * stamp its models carry resolves inside its own region (issue #75).\n *\n * The route id first, then the model identity, de-duplicated — route-first so\n * `profiles()` opens with the route the adapter actually serves. CN yields one\n * key (`workbuddy` is both its route and the shared identity) and the global\n * region yields two (`workbuddy-global`, `workbuddy`); the two regions\n * therefore publish different key sets, and each must publish the shared one.\n *\n * `identity` is a parameter because {@link WorkBuddyAdapterOptions.modelIdentity}\n * can override the stamp: the keys have to follow the stamp, or a test that\n * stamps a per-region id would get the production keys back.\n */\nexport function modelIdentityKeysOf(\n  providerId: string | undefined,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set([providerId, identity].filter((key): key is string => key !== undefined))]\n}\n\n/** Region a provider route id belongs to. */\nexport function regionOfProvider(provider: string): WorkBuddyRegion | undefined {\n  for (const [region, id] of Object.entries(WORKBUDDY_PROVIDERS) as [WorkBuddyRegion, string][]) {\n    if (id === provider) return region\n  }\n  return undefined\n}\n\n/** Human-readable provider name, shown in the DSH model picker. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER_DISPLAY_NAMES: Readonly<Record<WorkBuddyRegion, string>> = {\n  cn: 'WorkBuddy',\n  global: 'WorkBuddy Global',\n}\n\n/** Provider idle ceiling while one stream read is outstanding. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000\n\n/**\n * Image-request budgets for the route's `ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile`.\n *\n * The three byte/pixel budgets are the dsh-llm-pi-ai defaults (`0.1.1-rc.2`\n * made them required on the resolved profile).\n *\n * The two COUNT fields arrived with DSH 0.2.2 (`0.2.2-rc.1`), where\n * `dsh-llm-pi-ai` began enforcing a per-request image OCCURRENCE limit\n * (`maxRequestImages`, its own default 50) and the offload step that trims it\n * (`imageOffloadCountQuantum`, default `min(10, maxRequestImages)`). Both are\n * REQUIRED on the resolved profile, so this route must state a value; the\n * value is the plugin's decision, not a copy of the host's.\n *\n * WHY NOT 50. Until 0.2.2 this route had NO occurrence limit at all: the\n * adapter only ever passed the byte budget down (`toPiContextWithImages`\n * spreads `maxImages` only when it is defined), so a long session could carry\n * any number of images as long as the base64 payload fit. Adopting the host's\n * default would therefore NARROW this plugin's behaviour as a silent side\n * effect of a dependency bump — the kind of change that shows up as a user's\n * image-heavy session suddenly failing with `IMAGE_OFFLOAD_REQUIRED`.\n *\n * So both values are deliberately pinned far above any real request: they are\n * a TYPE requirement satisfied with \"no practical limit\", which keeps today's\n * behaviour byte-identical. `maxRequestImages` is a count of image\n * occurrences and the upstream gateway caps a request far below this on its\n * own; `imageOffloadCountQuantum` must not exceed `maxRequestImages`\n * (`dsh-llm-pi-ai` validates exactly that), so it is pinned to the same\n * giant value rather than to 10.\n *\n * If the gateway's real per-request image cap ever gets measured, THAT is the\n * number to put in `maxRequestImages` — with a comment saying where it came\n * from. Do not \"borrow\" the host's 50: it belongs to the host's own pi-ai\n * routes, not to WorkBuddy.\n */\nconst REQUEST_IMAGE_BUDGETS = {\n  maxRequestImages: 1_000_000,\n  imageOffloadCountQuantum: 1_000_000,\n  maxRequestImageBytes: 20_971_520,\n  requestImagePixelBudget: 4_194_304,\n  requestImageMaxBytes: 1_048_576,\n} as const\n\n/**\n * Inert pi-ai auth plane. The workbuddy route authenticates only through the\n * shim shared secret resolved per request by `resolveApiKey`, so pi-ai's own\n * credential lifecycle and ambient discovery must never manufacture a\n * credential for it. `PiAiAdapterOptions.auth` is required since 0.1.1-rc.2;\n * every ambient question here answers \"nothing stored, nothing set\".\n */\nconst INERT_AUTH: { credentials: CredentialStore; authContext: AuthContext } = {\n  credentials: {\n    async read() { return undefined },\n    async list() { return [] },\n    async modify() {\n      throw new Error('dsh-connect-workbuddy: the workbuddy route has no pi-ai credential lifecycle')\n    },\n    async delete() {},\n  },\n  authContext: {\n    async env() { return undefined },\n    async fileExists() { return false },\n  },\n}\n\n/** No per-token pricing is knowable for a subscription quota; report zero. */\nconst NO_COST = { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 } as const\n\n/** Constructor dependencies. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyAdapterOptions {\n  shim: WorkBuddyShim\n  store: WorkBuddyCredentialStore\n  catalog: WorkBuddyCatalog\n  /** Provider route id this instance serves; defaults to the CN route. */\n  provider?: string\n  /** pi-ai provider name and profile display name; defaults to the CN name. */\n  displayName?: string\n  /**\n   * Model identity stamped on every model this route serves; defaults to\n   * {@link WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY}. Tests override it to model a transcript\n   * recorded by a host that still stamped per-region ids.\n   *\n   * It also decides the extra registry key this adapter publishes alongside\n   * its route (issue #75) — see {@link modelIdentityKeysOf}, which is what\n   * keeps an overridden stamp resolvable exactly like the production one.\n   */\n  modelIdentity?: string\n  /** Resolve the durable attachment service at request time, when present. */\n  resolveAttachments?: () => AttachmentStore | undefined\n  /**\n   * Resolve the host-side context-overflow rescue, when this host exposes one.\n   * Omitted (or resolving to `undefined`) leaves the adapter exactly as it was:\n   * a provider overflow is forwarded and the host's own recovery decides.\n   */\n  resolveContextRescue?: () => ContextRescue | undefined\n  /**\n   * Override the pi-ai generation gate (see {@link piAiRuntimeInfo}); tests\n   * inject both branches because this checkout only ever RESOLVES one of\n   * them. Production callers leave it unset.\n   */\n  piAiGeneration?: PiAiGeneration\n  /**\n   * Drop SAME-ROUTE foreign thinking blocks pi-ai would publish as prose\n   * (issue #147).\n   *\n   * CROSS-PROVIDER turns are dropped unconditionally: after a provider switch\n   * the host rebuilds the other provider's assistant turns as `dsh-foreign`,\n   * their unsigned thinking blocks can never be replayed, and pi-ai would\n   * otherwise flatten them into assistant prose that the WorkBuddy gateway\n   * refuses with 11128 (issue #163's measured scene). This switch only widens\n   * the repair to a model switch INSIDE one of this bundle's own routes.\n   *\n   * OFF by default for that same-route leg, and read LIVE (a getter, not a\n   * boolean) so the switch takes effect on the next request without reloading\n   * the plugin: turning it on mid-session is the whole point — the user sees\n   * the working-notes turn, flips the switch, and the next turn is answered\n   * normally.\n   */\n  dropForeignThinking?: () => boolean\n  /**\n   * Route B (opt-in): ask pi-ai to stamp the host's session id onto each request\n   * as session-affinity HEADERS, so the loopback shim can read it and give the\n   * upstream a stable, session-scoped conversation id (a domain-separated\n   * digest; the real session id never leaves the process).\n   *\n   * OFF by default: this changes headers the provider sends AND is only correct\n   * if the shim is loopback-only + bearer-gated (it is — see `src/shim.ts`). Read\n   * LIVE so the card toggle takes effect on the next request without a reload.\n   * See `docs/ROUTE-B-SESSION-ID.md`; no upstream mechanism is asserted.\n   */\n  sessionConversationId?: () => boolean\n  /**\n   * Strip EVERY historical thinking block before the request goes out (issue #213).\n   *\n   * OFF by default, and this one is not a styling choice: the model loses its own\n   * earlier reasoning from context, which is exactly what it was doing, so a user\n   * should turn this on deliberately — typically AFTER seeing a 11128 they can match\n   * to reasoning text. The reporter's evidence for that match is in #213.\n   *\n   * Read LIVE for the same reason `dropForeignThinking` is: the switch has to work\n   * on the next turn of the session that just failed, without a reload.\n   */\n  dropAllThinking?: () => boolean\n  /**\n   * Told which foreign thinking blocks a request dropped, so the reply can name\n   * the condition instead of leaving the user to conclude the model broke\n   * (issue #147 asks for at least a visible hint). Called at most once per\n   * request; failures are swallowed.\n   */\n  onForeignThinking?: (blocks: readonly ForeignThinkingBlock[]) => void\n  /**\n   * The per-model default thinking tier the user saved (issue #179), keyed by\n   * model id.\n   *\n   * A GETTER, not a captured map: the choice is a setting the user can change\n   * mid-session, and a captured copy would keep dispatching the old tier until\n   * a reload. Production reads the live region slot; tests pass a literal.\n   *\n   * An absent key means \"follow the upstream's own `defaultEffort`\" — see\n   * `src/default-effort.ts` for how that is resolved against the model's real\n   * vocabulary, which is what keeps a stale saved tier from ever reaching the\n   * wire as an `UNSUPPORTED_REASONING_EFFORT`.\n   */\n  defaultThinkingEfforts?: () => Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>\n}\n\n/** What {@link createWorkBuddyAdapter} hands back. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyAdapter {\n  adapter: PiAiAdapter\n  /** Rebuild the adapter's provider snapshot; call after a catalog update. */\n  invalidate: () => void\n}\n\n/**\n * One selector badge, in the shape the host's model directory accepts from\n * DSH 0.2.2 on (`LlmModelInfo.badge`).\n *\n * Declared LOCALLY for the same reason `listModels` uses the spread form: this\n * plugin's peer range still starts at `0.1.7-rc.1`, whose `LlmModelInfo` has no\n * such field, so importing the type would not compile there. The shape is three\n * fields and it is stable; on 0.1.x nothing reads the property.\n *\n * `text` is a table when the badge has per-language wording. `en` is REQUIRED\n * by the host's resolver — a table without it resolves to `undefined`.\n *\n * WHY THE TABLE BRANCH NAMES `en` EXPLICITLY. The host's own type is\n * `LocalizedText = string | { readonly en: string; readonly [locale: string]:\n * string }` (`@deepseek-ai/dsh-package-manifest`): a bare\n * `Record<string, string>` is NOT assignable to it, because the required `en`\n * exists only in the literal type. Declaring this locally as a loose record\n * compiled against the 0.1.7 declarations (where the plugin's field never met\n * the host's) and stopped compiling once devDeps moved to the 0.2.2 line —\n * the plugin was still producing the right runtime values, but the type could\n * no longer express the invariant it depends on. Spelling `en` out is not a\n * workaround for that error: it is the invariant (an `en`-less table makes the\n * chip vanish) stated where the compiler can check it.\n */\nexport interface LlmModelBadge {\n  text: string | ({ en: string } & Record<string, string>)\n  tone: 'neutral' | 'info' | 'success' | 'warning'\n}\n\nconst THINKING_LEVELS = ['minimal', 'low', 'medium', 'high', 'xhigh', 'max'] as const\ntype WorkBuddyThinkingLevel = typeof THINKING_LEVELS[number]\ntype WorkBuddyThinkingLevelMap = Partial<Record<'off' | WorkBuddyThinkingLevel, string | null>>\n\n/** pi-ai input modalities: images only when WorkBuddy advertises them. */\nexport function workBuddyModelInput(info: WorkBuddyModelInfo): ('text' | 'image')[] {\n  return info.multimodal === true ? ['text', 'image'] : ['text']\n}\n\n/**\n * DSH-facing display name: the model name plus the upstream credit multiplier,\n * spelled the way WorkBuddy's own selector does (`GLM-5.3 · x0.79`).\n *\n * Display-only by construction: every DSH-side join keys on the model id —\n * the selector's current choice (`provider` + `model`), the durable\n * `model/selection` / `request/header` session events, the agent default-model\n * settings, and the request wire (`model: <id>` reaching the shim). A model\n * without a parsed multiplier keeps its bare name; a zero multiplier shows\n * `x0.00`, matching WorkBuddy's rendering of free models.\n */\nexport function workBuddyDisplayName(info: WorkBuddyModelInfo): string {\n  const base = info.creditMultiplier === undefined\n    ? info.name\n    : `${info.name} · x${info.creditMultiplier.toFixed(2)}`\n  // Load does NOT ride on this string any more. The host renders the model\n  // name verbatim, and the plugin now draws the load badge in the host's own\n  // picker through the `conversation.input.model.label` slot\n  // (`src/client/WorkBuddyModelLabel.tsx`) — where it can be a COLOURED figure\n  // rather than a character whose colour belongs to the emoji font. Leaving\n  // the emoji here as well would show the same fact twice, in two visual\n  // languages. See `src/busy.ts`.\n  return base\n}\n\n/**\n * Map only levels advertised by WorkBuddy; undeclared DSH levels stay unavailable.\n *\n * `off` is offered EXACTLY when `canDisableThinking === true` and withheld\n * (mapped to `null`) otherwise. `null` is pi-ai's \"this level is not\n * supported\": `getSupportedThinkingLevels` filters on `mapped === null`, and\n * `openai-completions` reads `thinkingLevelMap?.off !== null` to decide whether\n * it may send a thinking-disabled request.\n *\n * This condition used to be inverted (`!== true` set the null), which locked\n * `off` OFF for precisely the models that CAN disable thinking while leaving it\n * ON for those that cannot. It stayed invisible because the only test probed\n * the `canDisableThinking: false` half, which the inverted form also passes.\n *\n * The value read here is already the EFFECTIVE one: the runtime catalog is\n * stamped by `withEffectiveOff()` before it reaches the adapter, so this is the\n * user's `off` selection when they made one — except for the ids on\n * `REFUSES_OFF_MODEL_IDS`, which the stamp forces to `false` no matter what the\n * user asked for (issue #83: this level, once ticked, also became the IMPLICIT\n * default for every request that carries no effort at all, which is how\n * compaction went to 100% failure). The table is re-checked here as well so the\n * wall does not depend on every caller remembering to stamp first.\n *\n * Absence is \"unknown\" and stays unoffered, matching how the rest of the plugin\n * treats undeclared capability.\n */\nexport function workBuddyThinkingLevelMap(info: WorkBuddyModelInfo): WorkBuddyThinkingLevelMap | undefined {\n  const supported = info.reasoning?.supportedEfforts?.filter((effort): effort is WorkBuddyThinkingLevel =>\n    (THINKING_LEVELS as readonly string[]).includes(effort),\n  )\n  if (supported === undefined || supported.length === 0) return undefined\n  const map: WorkBuddyThinkingLevelMap = Object.fromEntries(\n    THINKING_LEVELS.map(level => [level, supported.includes(level) ? level : null]),\n  )\n  if (info.reasoning?.canDisableThinking === true && !offRefused(info.id)) map.off = 'off'\n  else map.off = null\n  return map\n}\n\n/**\n * Build one pi-ai model descriptor pointing at the loopback shim.\n *\n * `identity` — not the route id — is what `provider` carries; see\n * {@link WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY} for why the two must differ. The route the\n * model is REACHED by is the registry key the adapter serves, while this\n * stamped field is what pi-ai resolves the provider BY (issue #75) — so every\n * key in {@link modelIdentityKeysOf} must be a key this route registers.\n */\nfunction toPiModel(\n  info: WorkBuddyModelInfo,\n  baseUrl: string,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  midConvoSystemMessages = false,\n  sessionAffinity = false,\n): Model<Api> {\n  const thinkingLevelMap = workBuddyThinkingLevelMap(info)\n  // Declaring this CHANGES THE BYTES we send, so it is not a free statement.\n  //\n  // `dsh-llm-pi-ai` derives the request's `systemPromptUpdate: 'in-history'`\n  // from exactly this flag (`conversationUpdates()`), and the agent loop then\n  // APPENDS a changed system prompt after the cached history instead of\n  // rewriting node 0 — a mid-conversation `system` message goes out AS `system`\n  // instead of being folded into a `<system-reminder>` user turn. That is what\n  // preserves the upstream prefix cache across a tool-schema change (a plugin\n  // installed, an MCP server added, a skill edited), which otherwise re-billed\n  // the whole context at full price — tens of credits on one turn of a long\n  // session.\n  //\n  // The catch: the appended prompt is only obeyed if the gateway reads the\n  // LATEST `system` message regardless of position. Measured on THIS gateway\n  // (CN region): `system(S1) + history + system(S2) + ask` answered from S2, and\n  // the head stayed reusable (49% → 93% on the repeat). NOT measured on the\n  // international gateway, which is a different upstream — so this stays CN-only\n  // until someone measures it there. The failure mode is not a wasted credit: a\n  // gateway that reads only the head would keep obeying the OLD prompt, silently.\n  // Hence a parameter rather than an unconditional stamp.\n  const compat = {\n    ...midConvoSystemMessages\n      ? { supportsReasoningEffort: thinkingLevelMap !== undefined, supportsMidConvoSystemMessages: true }\n      : { supportsReasoningEffort: thinkingLevelMap !== undefined },\n    // Route B (opt-in): pi-ai's `createClient` reads these two keys and, when a\n    // session id is present on the request, stamps `session_id` /\n    // `x-client-request-id` / `x-session-affinity` on the HTTP request to the\n    // LOOPBACK shim. The shim — not pi-ai — is the only place that turns that\n    // value into an upstream id, and it uses a domain-separated digest.\n    //\n    // Both keys are absent in the default shape, so `getCompat` keeps pi-ai's\n    // detected default (`sendSessionAffinityHeaders: false`) and NOTHING changes\n    // on the wire for existing users. The value is a captured boolean at model\n    // construction (models are rebuilt on catalog refresh); the live getter on\n    // the adapter option decides WHICH model shape `buildModels` produces.\n    ...sessionAffinity\n      ? { sendSessionAffinityHeaders: true, sessionAffinityFormat: 'openai' as const }\n      : {},\n  }\n  return {\n    id: info.id,\n    name: workBuddyDisplayName(info),\n    api: 'openai-completions',\n    provider: identity,\n    baseUrl,\n    input: workBuddyModelInput(info),\n    cost: NO_COST,\n    contextWindow: info.contextWindow,\n    maxTokens: info.maxTokens,\n    reasoning: thinkingLevelMap !== undefined,\n    ...thinkingLevelMap === undefined ? {} : { thinkingLevelMap },\n    compat,\n  } as unknown as Model<Api>\n}\n\n/**\n * Rewrite a pi-ai 0.87-shaped transcript into the 0.85 shape this plugin's\n * pi-ai understands — prompt AND tools.\n *\n * WHY THIS EXISTS (issue #24). pi-ai 0.87's `normalizeContext` moves the system\n * prompt INTO `messages` as `{ role: 'system', content: '<string>' }`. pi-ai\n * 0.85's own `Message` union has no `system` variant\n * (`UserMessage | AssistantMessage | ToolResultMessage`), so its\n * `estimateMessageTokens` has no branch for one: `for (const block of\n * message.content)` iterates the CONTENT STRING character by character, `block`\n * is a single character, and `block.name.length` throws\n * `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'length')`.\n *\n * That crash happens inside the library, in `buildBaseOptions ->\n * clampMaxTokensToContext -> estimateContextTokens`, i.e. BEFORE any request is\n * built — so every model fails instantly and no upstream traffic is sent. A\n * host running 0.87 hands us the normalized transcript while our own provider\n * is 0.85, and the plugin cannot patch the library. What it CAN do is not hand\n * a 0.87 transcript to a 0.85 API object.\n *\n * The 0.87 transcript carries MORE than the prompt on that leading system\n * message: `toolsAdded` (and `toolsRemoved`) are the ONLY place the tool\n * declarations live — `normalizeContext` deleted the top-level `tools` field,\n * and 0.85's api reads tools ONLY from `context.tools`. So folding the text\n * without promoting the tool state would trade issue #24's loud crash for\n * issue #26's silent one: models that can never emit a `toolCall` because the\n * request never declared any tools. The fold here promotes both, replaying\n * every system message's `toolsRemoved`/`toolsAdded` in order — exactly the\n * merge pi-ai 0.87's own `getCurrentTools` performs — and leaves an existing\n * top-level `tools` array untouched.\n *\n * Deliberately SHAPE-based within the legacy branch, not version-based: it\n * asks \"does this context carry a system message inside `messages`?\" rather\n * than \"which pi-ai version is loaded?\". A version check would be wrong the\n * moment either side moves. Which BRANCH runs at all IS generation-gated —\n * see {@link withLegacyContext}: a modern (0.87+) api consumes this very\n * transcript natively, and folding for it would destroy its only carrier of\n * prompt and tools (issues #25/#26).\n *\n * Returns the SAME object when there is nothing to adapt — in particular when\n * `systemPrompt` is already set (the native 0.85 shape), so the ordinary path\n * is byte-for-byte untouched.\n *\n * Two guards beyond the 0.87 fold, both about a request reaching the upstream\n * with NO system message — which the international gateway refuses outright\n * (business code 11128) while the domestic one tolerates:\n *\n * - an EMPTY `systemPrompt` is treated as absent rather than \"already set\".\n *   pi-ai emits the prompt under `if (context.systemPrompt)`, so an empty one\n *   produces no system message at all and pi-ai demotes any `system` entry still\n *   in `messages` to `user`. Falling through here lets a real system message\n *   that IS present in `messages` be folded up and preserved.\n * - when nothing supplies a prompt, {@link WORKBUDDY_FALLBACK_SYSTEM_PROMPT} is\n *   used, because a placeholder beats a guaranteed 400. A system message whose\n *   text is empty but which carries `toolsAdded` still folds its TOOLS up; the\n *   placeholder for the missing prompt then comes from `ensureSystemHead` at\n *   the wire (the last of the three layers).\n *\n * LEGACY-ONLY. This is the inverse direction from {@link foldModernContext}:\n * it produces the FLAT `{systemPrompt, tools, messages}` shape that ONLY\n * pi-ai 0.85 reads. Never hand its result to a 1.x api — 1.x reads tools from\n * `messages[].toolsAdded` and would see none (issues #25/#26). Type note: the\n * parameter is the 0.85 `Context`; pi-ai 1.1.0 types the api's second parameter\n * as `TranscriptContext`, so this function cannot be called with 1.x typings —\n * that is intentional, and it is why the modern branch below does not touch it.\n */\nexport function adaptLegacyPiAiContext(context: Context): Context {\n  if (context === null || typeof context !== 'object') return context\n  // Truthiness, not `!== undefined`: see the empty-prompt guard above.\n  if (context.systemPrompt) return context\n  const messages = Array.isArray(context.messages) ? context.messages : undefined\n  if (messages === undefined) return context\n\n  const systemTexts: string[] = []\n  const rest: Message[] = []\n  const toolState = new Map<string, NonNullable<Context['tools']>[number]>()\n  let sawSystemMessage = false\n  for (const message of messages) {\n    const role = (message as { role?: unknown } | null)?.role\n    if (role !== 'system') {\n      rest.push(message)\n      continue\n    }\n    sawSystemMessage = true\n    const text = systemTextOf(message)\n    if (text !== '') systemTexts.push(text)\n    replayToolState(message, toolState)\n  }\n\n  const head = rest[0] as { role?: unknown } | undefined\n  const needsFallback = !sawSystemMessage\n    && head !== undefined\n    && head.role !== 'system'\n  if (!sawSystemMessage && !needsFallback) return context\n\n  // Dropped from `messages` even when no text could be extracted: leaving it\n  // there is precisely what crashes 0.85, and a system entry with no text has\n  // nothing to lose. `systemPrompt` stays ABSENT rather than '' so a provider\n  // never receives an empty system message.\n  const systemPrompt = systemTexts.length > 0\n    ? systemTexts.join('\\n\\n')\n    : sawSystemMessage ? undefined : WORKBUDDY_FALLBACK_SYSTEM_PROMPT\n\n  // Only a context with NO top-level tools gets the promoted set: when the\n  // caller supplied one, it is authoritative for their shape.\n  const tools = Array.isArray(context.tools)\n    ? undefined\n    : [...toolState.values()]\n\n  return {\n    ...context,\n    ...systemPrompt === undefined ? {} : { systemPrompt },\n    ...tools !== undefined && tools.length > 0 ? { tools } : {},\n    messages: rest,\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Replay one 0.87 system message's tool deltas into `state`, the same merge\n * pi-ai 0.87's `getCurrentTools` performs: removals first, then additions,\n * keyed by name so a mid-conversation redeclaration replaces the original.\n */\nfunction replayToolState(message: Message, state: Map<string, NonNullable<Context['tools']>[number]>): void {\n  const removed = (message as { toolsRemoved?: unknown }).toolsRemoved\n  if (Array.isArray(removed)) {\n    for (const tool of removed) {\n      const name = (tool as { name?: unknown } | null)?.name\n      if (typeof name === 'string') state.delete(name)\n    }\n  }\n  const added = (message as { toolsAdded?: unknown }).toolsAdded\n  if (Array.isArray(added)) {\n    for (const tool of added) {\n      const name = (tool as { name?: unknown } | null)?.name\n      if (typeof name === 'string') state.set(name, tool as NonNullable<Context['tools']>[number])\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** The text of one system message, whether it is a string or text blocks. */\nfunction systemTextOf(message: Message): string {\n  const content = (message as { content?: unknown }).content\n  if (typeof content === 'string') return content\n  if (!Array.isArray(content)) return ''\n  const texts: string[] = []\n  for (const block of content) {\n    if (typeof block !== 'object' || block === null) continue\n    const candidate = block as { type?: unknown, text?: unknown }\n    if (candidate.type === 'text' && typeof candidate.text === 'string') texts.push(candidate.text)\n  }\n  return texts.join('\\n\\n')\n}\n\n/**\n * Fold a RAW context into the normalized transcript the modern generation\n * consumes: a leading `system` message carrying the prompt AND the tool\n * declarations in its `toolsAdded` field.\n *\n * WHY THIS IS NEEDED AT ALL (the 0.2.1-rc.1 regression). This used to be\n * unnecessary: the modern branch returned pi-ai's api UNWRAPPED, on the belief\n * that the modern generation normalized for itself. It does not.\n * `dsh-llm-pi-ai` — both the 0.1.7 and the 0.2.1 lines — hands every provider a\n * RAW envelope (`piContext()` builds `{systemPrompt, messages, tools}` and\n * never calls `normalizeContext`), while:\n *\n *   - pi-ai 0.85.x READS that raw shape (its request builder consults\n *     `context.systemPrompt` / `context.tools` directly), and\n *   - pi-ai 1.x READS ONLY `context.messages` (it resolves the transcript and\n *     collapses system messages; the top-level fields are consulted nowhere).\n *\n * On pi-ai 1.x a pass-through request therefore went upstream with NO system\n * prompt and NO tool declarations, silently: the model stopped calling tools,\n * and the domestic gateway tolerated the prompt-less body. Same failure class\n * as issues #24/#25/#26, reached from the opposite side — 4.4.2 fixed \"1.x\n * misjudged as legacy and folded\"; this fixes \"1.x judged modern and NOT\n * folded, though nothing had normalized\".\n *\n * Built inline rather than by importing pi-ai's `normalizeContext`, which does\n * not exist in 0.85.x: a static import would be `undefined` there, and this\n * module deliberately never imports pi-ai statically (see `pi-ai-runtime.ts` —\n * that doctrine is what lets the standalone `bin` run in a profile where the\n * peer is never installed). The folded shape matches what `normalizeContext`\n * produces for the same input: `{role:'system', content, toolsAdded, timestamp:0}`.\n *\n * The top-level `systemPrompt`/`tools` fields are DROPPED, exactly as\n * `normalizeContext` drops them, so a raw transcript and an already-normalized\n * one converge on the same object.\n */\nfunction foldModernContext(context: Context): Context {\n  const prompt = context.systemPrompt\n  const tools = context.tools\n  const hasPrompt = prompt !== undefined && prompt !== ''\n  const hasTools = Array.isArray(tools) && tools.length > 0\n  // Nothing to fold: an already-normalized transcript (its system message is in\n  // `messages` already). Folding again would prepend a SECOND, empty one.\n  if (!hasPrompt && !hasTools) return context\n  const system = {\n    role: 'system',\n    content: prompt ?? '',\n    ...(hasTools ? { toolsAdded: tools } : {}),\n    timestamp: 0,\n  } as unknown as Message\n  const { systemPrompt: _prompt, tools: _tools, ...rest } = context as Context & {\n    systemPrompt?: string\n    tools?: Context['tools']\n  }\n  return { ...rest, messages: [system, ...context.messages] } as Context\n}\n\n/**\n * Wrap one pi-ai API module so both stream entry points receive a context this\n * generation can actually consume.\n *\n * The gate is the plugin's own module, resolved via {@link piAiRuntimeInfo}\n * (manifest version of the copy this plugin resolves — no pi-ai import):\n *\n * - `legacy` (0.85) wants the flat `{systemPrompt, tools, messages}` shape, so\n *   a normalized transcript is UN-folded by {@link adaptLegacyPiAiContext}.\n * - `modern` (0.87+, incl. 1.x) wants the normalized transcript, so a raw\n *   envelope is FOLDED by {@link foldModernContext}. Passing it through\n *   unnormalized is what silently deleted the prompt and the tools on pi-ai\n *   1.x — the regression this branch now prevents.\n *\n * Both branches are idempotent, so a future seam that starts normalizing on its\n * own side changes nothing here.\n *\n * The deferred-fetch entry points take no context and are passed through as-is\n * in both branches.\n */\n/**\n * Present an adapted context as the type the api's parameter currently expects.\n *\n * pi-ai 1.x BRANDS its provider-facing context (`TranscriptContext`, a\n * `unique symbol` field) precisely so a raw `Context` cannot reach provider\n * code by accident; the brand's stated sole producer is `normalizeContext()`.\n * On a 1.x typings surface BOTH branches below receive that branded type, so\n * merely returning a plain `Context` fails `tsc` with TS2345 — an error that\n * only appears when the peer TYPES are 1.x, which is why the 0.85 checkout\n * never saw it.\n *\n * The cast is a deliberate, documented assertion of exactly what the brand\n * protects, and it is true after the call site has done the work:\n *\n * - modern: {@link foldModernContext} has already performed\n *   `normalizeContext`'s job — prompt and tools live on the leading system\n *   message, top-level fields gone;\n * - legacy: {@link adaptLegacyPiAiContext} produced the FLAT 0.85 shape, which\n *   is only ever observed on a 0.85 checkout, where this type is a plain\n *   `Context` and the cast is an upcast.\n *\n * Do NOT \"fix\" this by importing `normalizeContext`: it does not exist in\n * 0.85, so that import would be `undefined` at runtime on the very hosts this\n * code must keep working — the same reason `pi-ai-runtime.ts` documents why\n * this module never imports pi-ai statically.\n */\nfunction transcript<T>(context: Context): T {\n  return context as unknown as T\n}\n\n/**\n * Rewrite every historical assistant message to the identity this adapter stamps.\n *\n * ISSUE #102. pi-ai replays a thinking block as `thinking` only when the message\n * that produced it names the same `provider` / `api` / `model` as the request in\n * flight (`utils/transform-messages.js`, the `isSameModel` branch); everything\n * else is flattened to `{type:'text', text: thinking}` — or, since pi-ai 1.3,\n * discarded outright. Either way the block stops being reasoning.\n *\n * The durable transcript the HOST hands back does not carry the identity. The\n * agent loop writes `source.provider = request.provider`, and `request.provider`\n * is the ROUTE id (`workbuddy` / `workbuddy-global`), while this adapter stamps\n * `provider: WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY` on the model. So:\n *\n *   - CN sessions survive by COINCIDENCE: its route id happens to equal the\n *     identity (`workbuddy` === `workbuddy`).\n *   - GLOBAL sessions never match (`workbuddy-global` !== `workbuddy`), so every\n *     historical thinking block is downgraded on EVERY turn — not only when the\n *     user switches regions, which is what #59's write-up assumed.\n *\n * The model then sees a context in which all of its own past reasoning was\n * plain assistant text, and writes its new reasoning into `text` to match. That\n * is the reported symptom: reasoning collapses to a character or two while the\n * answer channel fills with working notes.\n *\n * WHY REWRITING IS SAFE. Every message here was produced by THIS plugin, in THIS\n * region — the transcript never contains another provider's assistant turns\n * (`dsh-llm` splits history by provider before the API sees it). The identity is\n * already the field this adapter stamps on the models it serves, and it resolves\n * as a registry key in both regions (issue #75). Nothing about routing, billing\n * or the destination changes: `baseUrl` comes from the model, not from the\n * message.\n *\n * `provider` alone is NOT enough, because the HOST drops the message to\n * `api: 'dsh-foreign'` first. `dsh-llm-pi-ai`'s `replayedAssistant` rejects a\n * replay whose recorded provider differs from `source.provider` and falls back\n * to `foreignAssistant()`, which stamps `api: 'dsh-foreign'` and takes\n * `provider` from `source.provider` — the ROUTE id. That is why the provider\n * mismatch is not merely cosmetic: it also costs the message its native api, and\n * `isSameModel` then fails on the SECOND comparison even after `provider` is\n * repaired. Measured on a real 0.2.1-rc.2 global session, aligning `provider`\n * only still flattened a 1514-character thinking block into 1514 characters of\n * text; aligning both restored `thinking`.\n *\n * So `api` is repaired too, but ONLY from the one value that proves it is a\n * host fallback and not this plugin's own wire format: `dsh-foreign`. Any other\n * api is left exactly as it is.\n *\n * WHY THAT IS NOT FORGING A SIGNATURE. A `foreignAssistant()` message carries\n * thinking blocks with NO `thinkingSignature` (`dsh-llm-pi-ai` builds them as\n * `{type:'thinking', thinking}`), and pi-ai's request builder only emits\n * `reasoning_content` when a signature exists\n * (`api/openai-completions.js`, the `if (signature && isOpenAICompletionsReasoningField(...))`\n * branch). The block is therefore OMITTED from the request, never replayed as\n * signed reasoning — the same thing that happens today, minus the visible text.\n * The message's own `api` field is not read by the request builder at all\n * (grep: only `model.api` is), so this changes no wire shape.\n *\n * A genuinely different model still degrades, because `isSameModel` requires the\n * model id to match and this function deliberately does not rewrite it: the\n * message really was produced by another model. {@link dropForeignThinking}\n * covers that leg separately, through its `requestModelId` argument.\n *\n * Returns the same object when nothing changed, so the common case (an\n * already-normalized transcript) allocates nothing.\n */\nexport function alignAssistantIdentities(\n  context: Context,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  ownedRoutes: readonly string[] = [WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER, WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER],\n): Context {\n  const messages = context.messages\n  if (!Array.isArray(messages) || messages.length === 0) return context\n  // ISSUE #111: THE MESSAGE MUST BELONG TO THIS PLUGIN FIRST. The docblock\n  // above used to claim `dsh-llm` splits history by provider, so a transcript\n  // reaching this adapter could only contain this plugin's own turns. That is\n  // NOT what the host does: `forAdapter()` (dsh-llm/lib/index.js:2151) only\n  // STRIPS `replayState` from a message whose `source.provider` belongs to\n  // another adapter — it keeps the message in the list. So a session that\n  // switched providers (the user goes from DeepSeek official to WorkBuddy, or\n  // back) hands this adapter assistant turns that this plugin never produced,\n  // and the old code rewrote THEIR `provider` to `workbuddy` and their `api`\n  // to `openai-completions`.\n  //\n  // That is worse than cosmetic. It relabels a foreign turn as this plugin's\n  // own model output, so pi-ai's `isSameModel` compares it against the request\n  // and can replay its reasoning as if this model had produced it. It also\n  // makes the transcript claim a provenance that is false — the host's own\n  // `forAdapter` went out of its way to clear `replayState` precisely so a\n  // foreign turn would NOT be replayed, and rewriting the identity handed that\n  // turn back through the door `forAdapter` had just closed.\n  //\n  // So the rewrite is scoped to the routes this bundle owns. A message from any\n  // other provider is left EXACTLY as the host built it: its `api` stays\n  // `dsh-foreign`, which is the host's honest marker for \"not this adapter's\n  // wire format\", and its `provider` stays whatever that adapter registered.\n  const owned = new Set(ownedRoutes)\n  let changed = false\n  const next = messages.map(message => {\n    if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return message\n    const record = message as unknown as Record<string, unknown>\n    if (record['role'] !== 'assistant') return message\n    const provider = record['provider']\n    // A foreign turn keeps its own identity. `identity` is accepted here too\n    // because the identity IS this plugin's stamp: a message already carrying\n    // it is ours by definition (that is the CN case, where route and identity\n    // are the same string).\n    if (typeof provider !== 'string' || !owned.has(provider) && provider !== identity) return message\n    const providerAligned = provider === identity\n    // Only the host's own fallback marker is repaired; every other api is the\n    // shape this plugin really sends and must be preserved verbatim.\n    const apiAligned = record['api'] !== HOST_FOREIGN_API\n    if (providerAligned && apiAligned) return message\n    changed = true\n    const aligned = { ...record }\n    if (!providerAligned) aligned['provider'] = identity\n    if (!apiAligned) aligned['api'] = 'openai-completions'\n    return aligned as unknown as typeof message\n  })\n  return changed ? { ...context, messages: next } : context\n}\n\n/**\n * One foreign thinking block that pi-ai would publish as PROSE, and the reason it\n * would (issue #147).\n *\n * `variant` is not cosmetic: the two are handled by the same repair but they\n * mean different things, and the card explains them differently.\n *\n * - `flattened`: `stopReason` is a normal completion, so `transform-messages`\n *   turns the block into `{type:'text', text: thinking}` — the whole reasoning\n *   body lands in the visible answer. This is the disposition up to pi-ai 1.2;\n *   see the note on the field below for what 1.3+ does with it.\n * - `dropped`: `stopReason` is `error`/`aborted`, so the entire message is\n *   skipped; nothing reaches the wire and nothing reaches the user. It is\n *   reported anyway, because it still costs context: the model's answer to\n *   \"why did my last turn say nothing\" is this line, and a caller cannot see it\n *   from the transcript.\n *\n * ⚠️ On pi-ai 1.3+ the `flattened` label is historical, not predictive: the\n * library discards a foreign block instead of rewriting it, so the \"prose\n * leak\" this name describes can no longer happen there. The label is kept\n * because it is what the CALLER asks about (\"was this reasoning about to\n * become my answer?\") and because the repair — which is this plugin's own\n * guarantee, independent of whichever pi-ai a user resolves — is still the\n * thing that decides.\n */\nexport interface ForeignThinkingBlock {\n  /** Index of the message in the context's `messages`. */\n  messageIndex: number\n  /** Index of the block within that message's `content`. */\n  blockIndex: number\n  /** How many characters of reasoning the block carried. */\n  characters: number\n  /** Whether pi-ai would flatten it into prose or drop the whole message. */\n  variant: 'flattened' | 'dropped'\n}\n\n/** Read `content` as a block array without assuming the shape. */\nfunction blocksOf(message: unknown): unknown[] {\n  if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return []\n  const content = (message as Record<string, unknown>)['content']\n  return Array.isArray(content) ? content : []\n}\n\n/**\n * Whether this assistant message's identity proves a DIFFERENT model produced it.\n *\n * `requestModelId` closes the THIRD leg of pi-ai's own predicate. `isSameModel`\n * is `provider === … && api === … && model === model.id`\n * (`transform-messages.js`, and its line numbers move between releases, so this\n * file names the symbol); this function used to compare only the first two. The consequence was a blind spot the size of one whole route: a session\n * that switches MODELS inside one region — `hy3` → `deepseek-v4.1-flash`, both\n * served by this bundle, both stamped `provider: workbuddy` — keeps the provider\n * and api legs equal, so nothing here noticed, while pi-ai flattened every\n * historical thinking block into the answer channel exactly as in issue #147.\n * The user-visible symptom is #147's, with none of its diagnostics.\n *\n * Passing `undefined` keeps the provider/api-only behaviour, which is what the\n * read-only surfaces and the older call sites expect. Production passes the id\n * of the model the request is actually addressed to.\n */\nfunction isForeignAssistant(\n  message: unknown,\n  identity: string,\n  requestModelId?: string,\n): boolean {\n  if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return false\n  const record = message as Record<string, unknown>\n  if (record['role'] !== 'assistant') return false\n  // The host's own fallback marker beats everything: `foreignAssistant()` sets\n  // it precisely to say \"this is not the wire format the current request uses\".\n  if (record['api'] === HOST_FOREIGN_API) return true\n  const provider = record['provider']\n  if (typeof provider !== 'string' || provider !== identity\n    && provider !== WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER && provider !== WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER) return true\n  // Model leg. A message this bundle produced still degrades when the request\n  // names a different model id, because that is what `isSameModel` compares.\n  // `undefined` on either side means \"cannot compare\": stay silent rather than\n  // report a block pi-ai would have kept.\n  if (requestModelId === undefined) return false\n  const model = record['model']\n  return typeof model === 'string' && model !== requestModelId\n}\n\n/**\n * Whether this assistant turn came from a DIFFERENT PROVIDER/route, rather than\n * from a different model on one of this bundle's own routes.\n *\n * WHY THIS DISTINCTION EXISTS (issue #163's live scene). A session switched\n * from `dsh-opencode-go` to a WorkBuddy route mid-turn. The host rebuilt the\n * other provider's assistant turns as `dsh-foreign`, and pi-ai rewrote their\n * unsigned `thinking` blocks into visible `text` — 14,410 / 4,225 / 5,138\n * characters in the measured scene. WorkBuddy's gateway scans every assistant\n * message and refused the turn with business code 11128; all three in-place\n * rescue rungs and three auto-`继续` attempts failed because the bytes never\n * changed.\n *\n * A cross-provider thinking block is different from a same-route model switch:\n * it carries no `thinkingSignature` (the host builds `foreignAssistant()`\n * without one), so it could never be replayed as reasoning. Its only possible\n * contribution to the wire is the prose pi-ai manufactures from it. Dropping it\n * is therefore a correctness repair, not a preference — and it must happen\n * even when the user never opted into {@link dropForeignThinking}.\n *\n * Same-route model switches (`hy3` → `deepseek-v4.1-flash`) stay behind the\n * explicit switch, because those turns WERE produced by this bundle and their\n * reasoning may be worth keeping in the transcript for the user to read.\n */\nfunction isCrossProviderAssistant(\n  message: unknown,\n  identity: string,\n  ownedRoutes: readonly string[],\n): boolean {\n  if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return false\n  const record = message as Record<string, unknown>\n  if (record['role'] !== 'assistant') return false\n  // The host's fallback marker is the strongest signal: `foreignAssistant()`\n  // sets it for a turn this adapter did not produce. After\n  // `alignAssistantIdentities`, a turn this bundle owns has had the marker\n  // repaired away, so a remaining marker means genuinely foreign.\n  if (record['api'] === HOST_FOREIGN_API) return true\n  const provider = record['provider']\n  if (typeof provider !== 'string') return true\n  return provider !== identity && !ownedRoutes.includes(provider)\n}\n\n/**\n * Every foreign thinking block pi-ai would turn into visible prose (issue #147).\n *\n * THE DEFECT. pi-ai keeps a historical `thinking` block only while\n * `isSameModel` holds — provider AND api AND model id all equal to the request.\n * For everything else, up to pi-ai 1.2, it did NOT